      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we need to complete the square for the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms. Here are the steps:\n\n1. **Group the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms:**\n   \\[\n   z = (x^2 + 6x) + (2y^2 - 4y) + 22\n   \\]\n\n2. **Complete the square for the \\(x\\) terms:**\n   \\[\n   x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   (x^2 + 6x) = (x + 3)^2 - 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Complete the square for the \\(y\\) terms:**\n   \\[\n   2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   (2y^2 - 4y) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute the completed squares back into the function:**\n   \\[\n   z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22\n   \\]\n   Simplify the constants:\n   \\[\n   z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11\n   \\]\n\n5. **Identify the minimum value:**\n   The expression \\((x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2\\) is a sum of squares, which is always non-negative and equals zero when both \\((x + 3)^2 = 0\\) and \\((y - 1)^2 = 0\\). This occurs when \\(x = -3\\) and \\(y = 1\\).\n\n   Therefore, the minimum value of \\(z\\) is:\n   \\[\n   z = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11\n   \\]\n\nThe minimum value of \\(z\\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
