      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we will first complete the square for both \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) terms.\n\nStarting with the \\( x \\) terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x^2 + 6x + 9) - 9 = (x + 3)^2 - 9. \\]\n\nNext, the \\( y \\) terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2(y^2 - 2y + 1) - 2 = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2. \\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22. \\]\nSimplify the constant terms:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11. \\]\n\nThe expression \\( (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 \\) is a sum of two squares, which is always non-negative and achieves its minimum value of 0 when both squares are zero. Therefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) occurs when:\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2(y - 1)^2 = 0. \\]\nThis implies:\n\\[ x + 3 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y - 1 = 0, \\]\nso:\n\\[ x = -3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = 1. \\]\n\nSubstitute \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11 = 0 + 2(1 - 1)^2 + 11 = 0 + 2 \\cdot 0 + 11 = 11. \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
