      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we can complete the square for both \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\nFirst, consider the \\( x \\)-terms: \\( x^2 + 6x \\). To complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\( x \\), which is 6, divide by 2 to get 3, and then square it to get 9. So, we can rewrite \\( x^2 + 6x \\) as:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9. \\]\n\nNext, consider the \\( y \\)-terms: \\( 2y^2 - 4y \\). To complete the square, we first factor out the 2, get \\( 2(y^2 - 2y) \\), take half of the coefficient of \\( y \\) (which is -2), divide by 2 to get -1, and then square it to get 1. So, we can rewrite \\( y^2 - 2y \\) as:\n\\[ y^2 - 2y = (y - 1)^2 - 1, \\]\nand thus:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2. \\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original expression:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22. \\]\nSimplify the constants:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11. \\]\n\nThe expression \\((x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2\\) is a sum of two squares, which is always non-negative and can be zero only if both squares are zero. Therefore, the minimum value of \\((x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2\\) is 0, which occurs when \\( x + 3 = 0 \\) and \\( y - 1 = 0 \\), or \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[ z = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11. \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
